OSD_File: no mkstemp() and no POSIX record locking on wasm32-wasip1.

Both sites get a new arm INSIDE the platform chain that is already there, never a parallel block
beside it: SetLock() and UnLock() gain `#elif defined(__wasi__)` between the Windows arm and
`#elif defined(POSIX)`, and BuildTemporary() gains one between the VMS arm and the mkstemp `#else`.
No existing arm could be widened instead. WASI shares neither Windows' LockFileEx, nor Solaris'
lockf(), nor SYSV's fcntl(), nor BSD's flock(), and it has no mkstemp() to share with the generic
branch.

--- The lock ---

wasi-libc has no file locking of any kind. Its <fcntl.h> declares struct flock and, under
__wasilibc_unmodified_upstream, defines none of F_RDLCK, F_WRLCK, F_UNLCK, F_SETLK or F_SETLKW; the
header's own comments say "WASI has no POSIX file locking". There is nothing to call and nothing to
emulate.

WHAT THE WASI BRANCH DOES: SetLock() records the requested lock in myLock and makes no system call.
UnLock() sets myLock back to OSD_NoLock.

myError is deliberately NOT set, and that is the part that matters for callers. OSD_File::Read(),
Write(), Seek() and Close() all open with `if (Failed()) { Perror(); }`, and OSD_Error::Perror()
raises. Recording a refusal in myError would therefore convert "this platform has no file locking"
into "the next read on this file throws", which is a worse answer than the absent lock and one no
caller asked for. Every caller of SetLock() instead gets Failed() == false, IsLocked() == true, and
no lock, which is the truth of the platform: a wasip1 instance is one process with one thread, so
there is no second holder for an advisory lock to exclude. The single in-tree consumer is
~OSD_File(), which calls UnLock() when IsLocked(); that pairing is preserved exactly.

A hand-rolled substitute is NOT used, and PR #2076's is why this is worth stating. It declared a
separate `static thread_local std::atomic_flag` inside SetLock() and another inside UnLock(), so
UnLock() cleared an object SetLock() had never touched. An in-process flag cannot implement an
inter-process advisory lock even when it is the same object.

--- The temporary file ---

wasi-libc declares no mkstemp(): <stdlib.h> keeps it inside __wasilibc_unmodified_upstream under
the comment "WASI has no temp directories".

WHAT THE WASI BRANCH DOES: the six placeholder characters of "/tmp/CSFXXXXXX" are filled from
getentropy(), which wasi-libc does declare and does define, and the file is created with
open(O_CREAT | O_EXCL | O_RDWR, 0600), retried up to 100 times. O_EXCL is the part of mkstemp() that
carries the guarantee and it is kept, so a name that already exists is never adopted.

On failure the result is the same as a failing mkstemp(): myFileChannel stays -1, fdopen(-1)
returns null, and the OSD_File is left unopened, so the next Write() throws
Standard_ProgramError("OSD_File::Write(): file is not open") rather than writing somewhere
unexpected. /tmp has to be a preopened directory in the host's WASI configuration or every open()
fails that way. wasi-osd-directory.patch on this branch already assumes the same /tmp for
OSD_Directory::BuildTemporary().

ONE WARNING THIS BRANCH LEAVES: -Wunused-private-field on OSD_File::ImperativeFlag, because only
the Windows and SYSV lock paths read it. The Solaris `#elif defined(POSIX)` branch has the same
property today, so the warning is a property of the member rather than of this patch.

Issue: https://github.com/SecondMouseAU/OCCTSwift/issues/2173

